Bash Notes
Arithmetic Expresssions
-------------------------

Arithmetic expressions: $(( ))

You don't need $ preceding variables.

When a value can't be parsed as a number (i.e. a string) then it will be treated as a 0.

The "$" when defining an arithmetic expression is optional but required on echo. (())

You can increment with ++.
Example:

i=0 
((i++))
echo "i = $i"

Output: i = 1 

More operators:
(( i++ ))
(( i-- ))
(( i *= 5 ))
(( i ** 2 ))
(( i /= 2 ))
(( i %= 2 ))
(( i -= 2 ))
(( i += 2 ))
(( i >>= 5 )) -shift operator (shifts the binary of 2 (00000010) 5 places to the right)
(( i <<= 5 )) -shift operator (shifts the binary of 2 (00000010) 5 places to the left)
(( i &= 2 ))
(( i ^= 2 ))
(( i |= 2 ))


Shift operations are called bitwise shifts.
Can do logical operators.

In this syntax you can do ternaries ( b ? a : b )

Arithmetic expressions return error codes.
Examples:

(( 2 + 2 ))
ehco $?
0

(( 2 - 2 ))
echo $?
1

Zeros come out as a failure.
Zero as in the answer of the math expression. Not the error code which when 0 means it was a success.

set -e makes bash exit when encountering an error.

Broken Example:

set -e
i=0
while ((i < 10)); do
	echo "i is $i"
	((i++))
done

Why this is broken: The return code of ((i++)) is a failure because the first time it runs the answer is 0 when it's 0++. Although it resolves to 1, 0++ is a post increment so the increment takes place after the fact.

Leading zeros can cause problems in arithmetic expressions because it is viewed as octals.
That means numbers cannot be above 8. so a=07 echo $((a)) results in 7, a=08 echo $((a)) results in an error, a=010 echo $((a)) results in 8.

We can define a base in instances like this to strip the leading zeros off. a=010 echo $((10#$a)) results in 10. The 10# defines that it is in a base of 10.


